export const DATA_34: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-041", num: 41, title: "Đề 041", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Careers in the Music Industry", paragraphs: ["Many young people dream of working in the music industry, imagining a life filled with concerts, recording studios, and creative excitement. While becoming a famous performer is what most people think of first, the music industry actually offers a wide variety of career paths. From sound engineers and music producers to managers, journalists, and marketing professionals, there are many different ways to build a rewarding career in music without ever stepping onto a stage.", "One of the most important roles behind the scenes is that of the music producer. Producers work closely with artists in recording studios to help shape the sound of a song or an entire album. They are responsible for making creative decisions about how instruments are arranged, how the vocals are recorded, and how the final mix comes together. A skilled producer can turn a good song into a great one by bringing out the best in the musicians they work with. Many famous producers have worked with dozens of artists throughout their careers and have had a major influence on the direction of popular music.", "Sound engineers are another essential part of any music production. While producers focus on the creative side, sound engineers handle the technical aspects of recording. Their job involves operating recording equipment, adjusting sound levels, and ensuring that the audio quality meets professional standards. Sound engineers must have a strong understanding of both music and technology, and many of them study at specialised colleges or universities before starting their careers. In live settings such as concerts and festivals, sound engineers are responsible for making sure the audience hears a clear and balanced performance.", "Beyond the studio, there are many other careers that support the music industry. Music managers, for example, play a vital role in guiding the careers of artists. A manager helps an artist plan their schedule, negotiate contracts, and make important career decisions. Without a good manager, even a talented musician may struggle to reach a wide audience. Music journalists and critics also contribute to the industry by writing reviews, interviews, and articles that help the public discover new music and understand the stories behind their favourite artists. Meanwhile, marketing and promotions professionals work to build an artist's brand and ensure that new releases reach as many listeners as possible through social media, streaming platforms, and advertising campaigns.", "For those interested in the business side of music, careers in record labels and music publishing can be very appealing. Record label employees work on everything from signing new artists to overseeing the production and distribution of albums. Music publishers, on the other hand, manage the rights to songs and ensure that artists receive payment whenever their music is used in films, television programmes, or advertisements. Understanding music law and contracts is essential for anyone working in these areas, which is why many professionals in this field have backgrounds in business or law.", "The music industry is competitive and constantly changing, especially with the rise of digital streaming and online platforms. However, for people who are passionate about music and willing to work hard, there are more opportunities than ever before. Whether your strengths lie in creativity, technology, business, or communication, there is likely a role in the music industry that suits your skills. The key is to explore different areas, gain experience through internships or volunteer work, and never stop learning about the industry you hope to join."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The music industry offers a wide range of career opportunities beyond performing on stage.", "The music industry is too competitive for most people to succeed in.", "Digital streaming has made it easier for new artists to become famous without a record label.", "Becoming a music producer is the most rewarding career path in the music industry."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following best describes the role of a music producer?", options: ["A producer makes creative decisions about how a song is arranged and mixed.", "A producer is responsible for writing contracts and negotiating deals for artists.", "A producer mainly performs live at concerts alongside the artist they work with.", "A producer handles the technical operation of recording equipment in the studio."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what must sound engineers have a strong understanding of?", options: ["Music history and the careers of famous recording artists.", "Both music and technology.", "Social media platforms and digital marketing strategies.", "Business law and music publishing rights."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"Their\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["Colleges and universities that offer music programmes.", "Music producers who work in recording studios.", "Musicians who perform at live concerts and festivals.", "Sound engineers who handle technical aspects of recording."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what would happen to a talented musician without a good manager?", options: ["The musician might find it difficult to reach a wide audience.", "The musician would be unable to write or record any new songs.", "The musician would have to give up performing at concerts.", "The musician would lose all rights to their own music."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a career that supports the music industry?", options: ["Marketing and promotions professional", "Music manager", "Music journalist or critic", "Music therapist"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"overseeing\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Funding", "Supervising", "Cancelling", "Performing"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the main responsibility of music publishers?", options: ["Writing reviews and articles to help the public discover new music.", "Managing the rights to songs and ensuring artists are paid when their music is used.", "Designing album covers and promotional materials for record labels.", "Organising concert tours and booking performance venues for artists."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"competitive\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Focused entirely on digital platforms and streaming services.", "Involving many people striving for success in the same field.", "Easy to enter if you have a passion for music.", "Requiring very little formal training or qualifications."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Based on the information in paragraphs 3 and 5, what can be inferred about careers in the music industry?", options: ["Only people who can play a musical instrument are suitable for technical or business roles.", "Many roles in the music industry require specialised education or professional knowledge.", "Working in the music industry is only possible for people who grew up in large cities.", "Most music industry jobs require workers to travel internationally on a regular basis."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Benefits of Sports", paragraphs: ["Participating in sports is one of the most popular activities for people of all ages around the world. Whether it is a casual game of football in the park, a competitive swimming race, or a regular yoga class, sport takes many forms and offers something different to everyone. Researchers, doctors, and educators have long agreed that taking part in physical activity on a regular basis brings a wide range of benefits — not only for the body but also for the mind and for social wellbeing.", "The physical benefits of sport are perhaps the most widely recognised. Regular exercise strengthens the heart and improves the efficiency of the cardiovascular system, reducing the risk of serious conditions such as heart disease, high blood pressure, and type 2 diabetes. These conditions are increasingly common in modern societies where many people spend long hours sitting at desks or in front of screens. Sport also helps maintain a healthy body weight, builds muscle strength, and improves flexibility and coordination. For children and teenagers in particular, regular physical activity during the growing years supports healthy bone development and can establish habits that carry into adult life.", "Beyond the physical, sport has significant benefits for mental health. Exercise triggers the release of endorphins — natural chemicals in the brain that create feelings of happiness and reduce stress. People who play sport regularly often report better sleep quality and higher levels of energy throughout the day. Sport can also serve as a powerful way to manage anxiety and depression. Studies have shown that even moderate exercise, such as a thirty-minute walk or a gentle swim, can have a noticeable positive effect on a person's mood and overall sense of wellbeing . For young people especially, sport provides a healthy outlet for emotions and can help build self-confidence and resilience.", "Sport is also a powerful tool for building social connections. Team sports such as basketball, volleyball, and rugby require players to work together, communicate effectively, and support one another — skills that are just as valuable off the field as they are on it. Its role in building teamwork and cooperation makes sport a natural environment for developing leadership qualities. Individual sports, meanwhile, teach self-discipline, patience, and the ability to handle both success and defeat with grace. Regardless of the format, sport regularly brings people from different backgrounds together, creating shared experiences and a sense of community that can bridge cultural and social divides.", "In schools and universities, sport plays an important role in students' overall development. Physical education programmes give young people the opportunity to explore different activities, discover their strengths, and learn the value of effort and persistence. Many educators believe that students who engage in regular sport perform better academically, as physical activity has been linked to improved concentration, memory, and classroom behaviour. Extracurricular sports teams also help students develop a sense of belonging and school identity, which can positively influence their motivation to attend and engage with their studies.", "Despite all these well-documented advantages, many people still struggle to make sport a regular part of their lives. Common barriers include a lack of time, limited access to facilities, financial costs, or simply a belief that sport is \"not for them.\" Addressing these obstacles requires effort from governments, schools, and communities to make sport more accessible and inclusive . When opportunities for physical activity are available and welcoming to everyone, the benefits extend beyond the individual to society as a whole — through lower healthcare costs, stronger communities, and a healthier, more productive population.", "In summary, the case for sport goes far beyond simply staying fit. Its contributions to physical health, mental wellbeing, social development, and academic performance make it one of the most valuable habits a person can cultivate at any stage of life. Encouraging wider participation in sport is therefore not merely a matter of personal choice but a genuine investment in the health and happiness of communities everywhere."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main topic of the passage?", options: ["The wide-ranging benefits of participating in sport for physical, mental, and social wellbeing.", "How governments and schools can increase student participation in physical education.", "The different types of competitive sports available to young people.", "The reasons why many people fail to exercise regularly despite knowing its advantages."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is mentioned as a physical benefit of regular sport?", options: ["It improves a person's ability to concentrate during work or study.", "It helps prevent serious health conditions such as heart disease and type 2 diabetes.", "It reduces feelings of anxiety and depression by releasing natural brain chemicals.", "It encourages people to spend less time in front of screens and more time outdoors."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what are endorphins?", options: ["Natural brain chemicals that produce feelings of happiness and lower stress.", "A type of exercise routine recommended for reducing stress.", "Specialised coaching techniques used to improve athletic performance.", "Habits formed through regular physical activity that last into adulthood."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"Its\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["Sport's capacity to develop teamwork and cooperation.", "The field or playing area where team sports are practised.", "The set of communication skills required for effective team performance.", "Basketball, as the team sport most commonly associated with leadership training."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a mental health benefit of sport?", options: ["Reduced feelings of anxiety and depression.", "Improved long-term memory and academic test scores.", "Better quality of sleep.", "Higher energy levels throughout the day."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how has regular physical activity been linked to academic performance?", options: ["It gives students more free time to study by reducing the hours spent on homework.", "It replaces traditional classroom instruction with more practical, hands-on learning.", "It has been connected to improvements in concentration, memory, and classroom behaviour.", "It teaches students to compete against one another, which motivates higher grades."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"wellbeing\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["The ability to perform well under pressure.", "The physical strength gained through regular training.", "A general state of health and happiness.", "A medical condition treated by exercise."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"inclusive\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Highly competitive and challenging for beginners.", "Limited to activities that require expensive equipment or facilities.", "Focused exclusively on professional or elite athletes.", "Open and welcoming to people of all backgrounds and abilities."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 4 and 6, what can be inferred about the broader social value of sport?", options: ["When sport is made accessible to everyone, its benefits in building cooperation and community extend beyond individuals to society as a whole.", "The social skills developed through sport are only relevant to professional athletes.", "Sport is most beneficial when played competitively rather than for recreation.", "Cultural and social differences make it difficult for sport to truly unite communities."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward sport as expressed throughout the passage?", options: ["Supportive and encouraging, presenting sport as a valuable investment in personal and community health.", "Cautious, suggesting that sport should only be recommended for people who are already physically healthy.", "Critical, arguing that the benefits of sport are often overstated by researchers and educators.", "Neutral, presenting both the advantages and disadvantages of sport without taking a clear position."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Hadza People and Their Traditional Lifestyle", paragraphs: ["Deep in the semi-arid landscape surrounding Lake Eyasi in northern Tanzania lives one of the world's last truly hunter-gatherer societies — the Hadza. With a population of roughly 1,200 to 1,500 individuals, only about 300 to 400 of them continue to live by hunting wild animals and foraging for plant foods in ways that have remained largely unchanged for tens of thousands of years. The Hadza are not related linguistically or genetically to any of their agricultural or pastoralist neighbors, a fact that underscores just how ancient and distinct their way of life truly is.", "The Hadza diet is extraordinarily diverse by modern standards. Men hunt a wide variety of game using handcrafted bows and poison-tipped arrows, targeting animals ranging from small birds to large ungulates such as zebra and buffalo. Women, meanwhile, are responsible for the bulk of daily caloric intake, spending long hours gathering tubers, berries, baobab fruit, and honey. Studies conducted by nutritional anthropologists have found that the Hadza consume over 600 different plant and animal species over the course of a year, a figure that stands in stark contrast to the highly processed diets of industrialized nations. Critically, the Hadza do not practice food storage — each day's meal is obtained and consumed within that same day, reflecting a profound trust in the landscape's continued abundance.", "Social organization among the Hadza is remarkably fluid. Camps typically consist of between 20 and 30 individuals, and membership shifts frequently as people move between groups to visit relatives, resolve conflicts, or follow seasonal food sources. There is no permanent leadership structure; decisions about where to move or what to hunt are made through open discussion among adults. [1] Anthropologists describe this system as \"immediate-return\" economics, meaning that effort invested in food-getting yields a direct and same-day reward, with no delayed gratification built into the social contract. [2] Ownership of material goods is strongly discouraged, and individuals who accumulate more than their fair share face social pressure to redistribute. [3] This egalitarian ethos extends even to gender relations; while men and women occupy different economic roles, neither sex is considered subordinate, and both participate openly in community decisions. [4]", "Language is another feature that sets the Hadza apart. They speak Hadzane, a language that incorporates click consonants — sounds produced by drawing air inward across the tongue or palate — yet it belongs to no established language family. Its origins remain one of Africa's great linguistic mysteries. Despite years of research, scholars have been unable to link Hadzane to Khoisan languages, Nilo-Saharan languages, or any other known grouping, suggesting that the Hadza may represent a population that has remained genetically and culturally isolated for an extraordinarily long period.", "The relationship between the Hadza and the outside world is increasingly fraught . Since the 1960s, repeated attempts by the Tanzanian government to settle the Hadza into permanent agricultural villages have largely failed; most Hadza who were relocated eventually returned to their foraging lifestyle. More recently, land encroachment by neighboring Datooga pastoralists and Iraqw farmers has dramatically reduced the territory available for hunting and gathering. Tourism, while providing some economic benefit, has also introduced new disruptions — some Hadza communities near popular safari routes have become semi-permanent performers of their own culture for visiting visitors, a development that many researchers view with concern.", "Nevertheless, the Hadza remain a source of remarkable scientific insight. Studies of their gut microbiome have revealed extraordinary bacterial diversity — far greater than that found in Western populations — that researchers believe is linked to their highly varied diet and frequent contact with soil and wild animals. This microbial richness may help explain the Hadza's notable resistance to a range of inflammatory and autoimmune diseases that are increasingly prevalent in industrialized societies. In this sense, the Hadza do not merely represent a relic of the past; they offer a living laboratory for understanding the biological conditions under which human beings evolved, and the costs of departing from them.", "The question of how long this lifestyle can survive is pressing. Younger Hadza are increasingly exposed to formal education, mobile phone networks, and market economies, and the appeal of these forces is difficult to resist. Researchers and advocacy groups argue that the most respectful and effective approach is not to freeze the Hadza in time, but to support their legal right to ancestral lands so that those who choose to continue foraging may do so freely. Whether that choice remains viable in another generation is a question that no one can yet answer with confidence."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of this passage?", options: ["To provide a comprehensive overview of the Hadza people, covering their lifestyle, culture, and the challenges they face", "To explain why the Hadza diet is superior to the diets of industrialized nations", "To argue that governments should stop trying to settle hunter-gatherer communities", "To trace the historical origins of the Hadzane language"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following best describes the Hadza approach to food?", options: ["They rely primarily on men to provide all daily nutritional needs through hunting", "They have recently adopted some agricultural techniques from neighboring communities", "They store excess food during seasons of abundance to prepare for shortages", "They consume a vast range of species daily and do not preserve food for future use"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Under this arrangement, no individual is expected to defer reward or accumulate resources on behalf of the group.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What makes the Hadzane language particularly significant to researchers?", options: ["It has recently been decoded using genetic evidence from Hadza populations", "It shares grammatical structures with Khoisan languages despite being geographically distant", "It cannot be classified within any recognized language family, making its origins unknown", "It is the oldest documented language in sub-Saharan Africa"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a pressure threatening the Hadza way of life?", options: ["Armed conflict with wildlife conservation organizations over hunting rights", "Government-led programs attempting to relocate Hadza into settled villages", "The commercialization of their cultural practices for tourist audiences", "Encroachment on their land by neighboring farming and herding communities"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Based on information in paragraph 6, researchers studying the Hadza gut microbiome suggest that their bacterial diversity is most directly linked to _______.", options: ["the absence of any formal medical treatment throughout their lives", "the combination of a highly varied diet and regular exposure to wild environments", "the physical demands of daily hunting and long-distance foraging", "their genetic isolation from neighboring populations over thousands of years"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"processed\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["preserved through traditional smoking or drying methods", "industrially manufactured and chemically altered", "carefully selected from local environments", "nutritionally balanced and scientifically designed"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 1 refers to _______.", options: ["researchers and anthropologists studying the Hadza", "the Hadza's agricultural and pastoralist neighbors", "all 1,200 to 1,500 members of the Hadza population", "the animals hunted by Hadza men in the Lake Eyasi region"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"fraught\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["carefully managed by local authorities", "deeply rooted in tradition", "gradually improving over time", "filled with tension and difficulty"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the final two paragraphs about the future of the Hadza people?", options: ["Younger Hadza generations are actively rejecting traditional practices in favor of urban lifestyles", "Scientific research into the Hadza microbiome will provide sufficient funding to protect their ancestral lands", "The survival of the Hadza foraging lifestyle depends more on securing land rights than on resisting all forms of outside contact", "The Hadza will inevitably abandon their traditional lifestyle within the next decade due to economic pressures"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Musical Creativity in the Age of AI", paragraphs: ["The emergence of artificial intelligence as a compositional tool has prompted one of the most searching debates in contemporary musicology: whether the act of musical creation is, at its core, a uniquely human capacity, or merely a sophisticated form of pattern recognition that machines may eventually replicate — and perhaps surpass. [1] Early AI music systems, such as David Cope's Experiments in Musical Intelligence developed in the 1980s, generated stylistic pastiches of composers like Bach and Chopin by analyzing and recombining structural patterns extracted from existing scores. [2] These outputs divided critics sharply: some dismissed them as elaborate mimicry devoid of genuine artistic intent, while others argued that if a listener cannot distinguish AI-generated music from human-composed work, the ontological distinction may be less significant than traditionally assumed. [3] What neither camp fully anticipated, however, was the speed at which the technology would evolve from rule-based imitation to generative systems capable of producing wholly original sonic landscapes. [4]", "Contemporary AI music platforms — among them Google's MusicLM, OpenAI's Jukebox, and a proliferating range of commercial tools — operate on deep learning architectures trained on vast corpora of recorded music. Unlike their predecessors, these systems do not merely interpolate between known stylistic templates; they are capable of synthesizing entirely novel combinations of timbre, harmony, rhythm, and structure in response to natural language prompts. A user may instruct the system to produce \"a melancholic cello piece reminiscent of late Romantic music but with jazz-inflected harmonics,\" and the model will generate an output that, to trained ears, bears credible resemblance to the described aesthetic. The implications for the music industry are profound and deeply unsettling to many practitioners.", "The concern most frequently voiced by professional composers and performers is not that AI will produce better music than humans, but that it will produce sufficiently good music at a fraction of the cost and time. In markets where background music for film, advertising, and digital content already commands a substantial share of total music revenue, the economic calculus is unambiguous: a platform licensing fee for unlimited AI-generated tracks is categorically cheaper than commissioning a human composer. The International Federation of the Phonographic Industry reported in 2023 that AI-generated content already constituted a measurable proportion of newly uploaded tracks on major streaming platforms, a figure that industry analysts project will grow exponentially over the coming decade.", "Defenders of AI as a creative partner rather than a competitor point to a different set of considerations. Composers such as Holly Herndon and Arca have incorporated machine learning models directly into their compositional processes, treating AI outputs as raw material to be shaped, rejected, or transformed through human aesthetic judgment. For these practitioners, the question of whether the AI \"understands\" what it is producing is beside the point; what matters is the expanded palette of sonic possibility that the technology affords. This position finds some theoretical support in philosophical accounts of creativity that locate the source of artistic value not in the inner experience of the creator but in the relational encounter between a work and its audience — a framework within which the origin of the generative process becomes secondary to its effect.", "Yet this optimistic framing has its own tensions. Critics note that AI systems trained on existing human music effectively commodify the creative labor of the artists whose work constitutes the training data, typically without consent or compensation. Several high-profile lawsuits filed in the United States and the European Union have challenged the legality of training large models on copyrighted material, and the outcomes of these cases are likely to reshape the regulatory landscape significantly. Moreover, the argument that AI serves merely as an instrument in the hands of a human artist elides a more uncomfortable possibility: that as these systems become more capable, the human contribution to the compositional process may diminish to the point where the distinction between \"AI-assisted\" and \"AI-generated\" music becomes functionally meaningless.", "At the deepest level, the debate about AI and musical creativity forces a reconsideration of what music is for. If its primary function is to produce pleasurable or emotionally resonant sound, then the provenance of that sound — whether human or algorithmic — may indeed be irrelevant to the listener's experience. But if music is understood as a form of communication between conscious beings, encoding lived experience, cultural memory, and embodied emotion, then the substitution of algorithmic generation for human expression arguably involves a category error of considerable magnitude. These two positions are not easily reconciled, and the discomfort they generate may be precisely the productive tension that the next generation of musicians and theorists will need to inhabit.", "What seems clear is that the relationship between human creativity and computational capability is not a zero-sum contest. The historical record of artistic practice suggests that new technologies — from the printing press to the electric guitar — have invariably expanded rather than extinguished the possibilities for human expression. Whether AI follows this pattern, or whether its particular form of generative power represents a qualitative departure from previous technological disruptions, remains genuinely open. The musicians and composers who will answer that question most convincingly are, in all likelihood, already at work."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'These early experiments revealed as much about human aesthetic bias as they did about the limits of computational imitation.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do contemporary AI music platforms differ from earlier AI music systems?", options: ["They are limited to imitating specific composers whose works have entered the public domain", "They rely on human composers to review and approve each generated output before release", "They generate entirely new musical combinations rather than recombining elements from existing stylistic models", "They require users to have advanced musical training in order to operate them effectively"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a concern raised by professional musicians regarding AI-generated music?", options: ["AI music may be produced at a substantially lower cost than hiring a human composer", "AI systems may eventually develop emotional intelligence that surpasses human composers", "AI-generated tracks are increasingly present on major streaming platforms", "Background music markets represent a significant portion of total music industry revenue"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the significance of mentioning composers like Holly Herndon and Arca in paragraph 4?", options: ["They demonstrate that AI tools have already replaced traditional compositional methods in mainstream music", "They illustrate that some practitioners view AI as a creative resource to be shaped by human judgment rather than as a rival", "They show that legal challenges against AI music platforms have not deterred professional musicians from using them", "They prove that AI-generated music can achieve commercial success without any human involvement"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"synthesizing\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["faithfully reproducing established compositional templates", "constructing new wholes by combining diverse and varied elements", "translating written instructions into standardized musical notation", "selecting the most commercially viable sounds from a fixed database"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What does the author suggest about the legal challenges mentioned in paragraph 5?", options: ["They have already resulted in major AI platforms withdrawing their music generation tools from the market", "They reflect a widespread public consensus that AI music tools should be banned entirely", "They are unlikely to succeed because existing copyright law does not apply to AI-generated content", "Their outcomes are expected to have a significant influence on how AI music technology is regulated going forward"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"commodify\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["to transform something into a product that can be commercially exploited", "to distribute creative content freely across digital platforms", "to formally acknowledge the artistic value of a creative work", "to legally protect intellectual property from unauthorized reproduction"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"its\" in the sentence \"what matters is the expanded palette of sonic possibility that the technology affords\" (paragraph 4) refers to _______.", options: ["the philosophical framework that locates artistic value in audience reception", "the human aesthetic judgment applied to AI-generated outputs", "the machine learning model incorporated into the compositional process", "the question of whether AI understands what it is producing"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following best describes the author's overall attitude toward AI's role in musical creativity?", options: ["Pessimistic, implying that AI will inevitably reduce human composers to irrelevance within the coming decade", "Analytically balanced, acknowledging both the creative potential and the unresolved ethical and aesthetic tensions that AI introduces", "Dismissive, suggesting that AI-generated music lacks the qualities that make human composition meaningful", "Enthusiastically supportive, arguing that AI will liberate musicians from technical constraints"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Based on the passage as a whole, what can be inferred about the author's view of historical comparisons between AI and earlier technologies such as the printing press or the electric guitar?", options: ["The author finds the comparison historically inaccurate because earlier technologies did not generate independent creative content", "The author rejects the comparison entirely, arguing that no previous technology has posed a comparable threat to artistic employment", "The author fully endorses this comparison, suggesting AI will follow exactly the same trajectory of expanding human creative possibilities", "The author considers the comparison useful but potentially insufficient, since AI's generative capacity may represent a fundamentally different kind of disruption"], answer: 3 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-061", num: 61, title: "Đề 061", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Vibrant Culture of Vietnamese Street Food", paragraphs: ["Walk down almost any street in a Vietnamese city at breakfast time and you will find the same irresistible scene: small plastic stools pulled up to low tables on the pavement, bowls of steaming soup carried out from tiny kitchens, and the rich smell of herbs, broth, and grilled meat drifting through the morning air. Street food is not simply a convenient way to eat in Vietnam — it is a living expression of the country's history, geography, and community values, woven into the daily rhythm of life in a way that no restaurant or supermarket could ever replace.", "The variety of Vietnamese street food is extraordinary, and much of it reflects the country's long and complex history. Vietnam stretches over 1,600 kilometers from north to south, and the cuisine changes dramatically along the way. In the north, dishes tend to be simpler and less sweet, with clear broths and a focus on fresh herbs. The central region, which was once home to the imperial court, is known for its elaborate and beautifully presented dishes. In the south, the food is generally sweeter and more heavily influenced by the ingredients and cooking styles brought by Chinese and Khmer communities. This regional diversity means that it is virtually impossible to describe Vietnamese street food as a single unified tradition.", "Among the most iconic street food dishes is pho — a fragrant noodle soup made with a slow-cooked broth of beef or chicken bones, rice noodles, and an array of fresh toppings such as bean sprouts, lime, chili, and fresh herbs. Although pho is now served in Vietnamese restaurants on every continent, its true spiritual home is the street. A bowl of pho prepared by a vendor who has spent decades perfecting the same recipe, served at a pavement stall before dawn, carries a depth of flavor and a sense of occasion that no formal dining room can easily reproduce. Locals often have a favorite vendor and will travel across their neighborhood specifically to eat at their stall rather than a closer alternative.", "Street food in Vietnam is also deeply connected to the social fabric of everyday life. Eating on the street is not considered informal or low-status — it is simply the most natural and enjoyable way to share a meal. Friends catch up over bowls of bun bo Hue; office workers grab a banh mi sandwich on their lunch break; grandparents take grandchildren to a familiar corner stall on Sunday mornings. The street food stall functions as a kind of neighborhood gathering point, a place where people from different backgrounds meet briefly as equals over a shared meal. In this sense, Vietnamese street food does far more than nourish the body — it nourishes the community itself.", "The people who prepare and sell street food are as important to its culture as the food itself. Many vendors have inherited their recipes from parents or grandparents, and the preparation of a dish like banh cuon — thin steamed rice rolls filled with minced pork and wood-ear mushrooms — can involve skills developed and refined over a lifetime. Some of the most respected street food vendors in cities like Hanoi and Ho Chi Minh City have become local legends, with loyal customers who have been eating at the same stall for thirty or forty years. The continuity between generations is something that regulars deeply value, and the disappearance of a well-loved vendor is often mourned like the loss of a neighborhood institution.", "In recent years, Vietnamese street food has attracted significant international attention. Food writers, travel bloggers, and television programs have brought dishes like pho, banh mi, and goi cuon — fresh spring rolls — to global audiences, and Vietnamese cuisine is now consistently ranked among the most popular in the world. This international recognition has brought both opportunities and challenges. On the positive side, it has generated new interest in Vietnamese culinary heritage and encouraged younger generations to take pride in traditions that might otherwise have seemed ordinary. On the less positive side, rising tourism and the pressure to standardize dishes for foreign tastes risk gradually eroding the local authenticity that makes street food so special in the first place.", "Despite these pressures, Vietnamese street food remains a remarkably resilient tradition. New vendors appear every year, experimenting with regional recipes or combining traditional flavors with modern techniques, while established stalls continue to serve the same dishes with the same dedication as always. For millions of Vietnamese people, eating on the street is not a nostalgic habit from the past but a living, evolving part of daily life — as relevant and satisfying today as it has ever been."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Vietnamese street food is a rich cultural tradition that reflects history, regional diversity, and community life, and continues to thrive today.", "The most important street food dish in Vietnam is pho, which has a history stretching back many centuries.", "Street food vendors in Vietnam are under serious threat from rising tourism and the pressure to change their recipes for foreign visitors.", "Vietnamese street food has become internationally famous and is now served in restaurants on every continent."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does street food in the north of Vietnam differ from food in the south?", options: ["Northern food is cooked over charcoal, while southern food is typically steamed or served raw.", "Northern food uses more spices and is generally hotter and more heavily seasoned than southern food.", "Northern food is more popular with international tourists, while southern food is preferred by local Vietnamese people.", "Northern food tends to be simpler and less sweet, while southern food is sweeter and more influenced by other cultures."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what are the main ingredients used in a bowl of pho?", options: ["Wheat noodles, a clear fish broth, and dried toppings including mushrooms and seaweed", "Glass noodles, a pork-based broth, pickled vegetables, and crushed peanuts", "Egg noodles, a spicy coconut broth, fresh vegetables, and crispy fried shallots", "Rice noodles, a slow-cooked bone broth, and fresh toppings such as bean sprouts and herbs"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is special about banh cuon vendors who have been selling their food for many years?", options: ["They have developed and refined their skills over a lifetime, often inheriting recipes passed down through generations.", "They have adapted their original recipes to meet the demands of international tourists visiting Vietnam.", "They have trained professionally at culinary schools and hold certificates in traditional Vietnamese cooking.", "They are employed by the city government to preserve traditional food culture in public spaces."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"their\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the formal dining rooms that try to recreate street food", "the fresh toppings served alongside a bowl of pho", "the local customers who travel across the neighborhood to eat", "the favorite vendor's pavement stall"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one negative consequence of the growing international fame of Vietnamese street food?", options: ["The pressure to adapt dishes for foreign tastes risks slowly reducing the local authenticity of street food.", "It has led to government regulations that restrict where and when street food vendors are allowed to operate.", "It has made Vietnamese ingredients more expensive, so local people can no longer afford to eat on the street.", "It has caused many younger Vietnamese people to lose interest in their own culinary traditions."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"nourish\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["to entertain or amuse a group of people", "to feed and sustain the health and strength of something", "to bring order and discipline to a social situation", "to record and preserve information about something"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"generated\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["reported accurately in the international media", "produced or created as a result of something", "transferred from one place to another", "reduced significantly over a short period of time"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an example of how street food connects people in Vietnamese communities?", options: ["Friends meeting to share a meal together at a street stall", "Grandparents taking their grandchildren to a familiar corner stall", "Local vendors donating food to community festivals on public holidays", "Office workers buying a sandwich during their lunch break"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's view of the future of Vietnamese street food?", options: ["The author believes that rising tourism will completely destroy the authenticity of Vietnamese street food within a generation.", "The author suggests that although real challenges exist, Vietnamese street food is resilient enough to continue evolving and thriving.", "The author implies that only older generations truly appreciate street food and that younger Vietnamese people are losing interest in it.", "The author is confident that Vietnamese street food will spread globally and eventually replace local food traditions in other countries."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Barriers to Foreign Language Acquisition Among Students", paragraphs: ["Learning a foreign language is widely regarded as one of the most rewarding intellectual endeavors a person can undertake. It opens doors to new cultures, broadens career opportunities, and has been shown to have measurable cognitive benefits, including improved memory, greater mental flexibility, and a delayed onset of age-related cognitive decline. Yet despite these well-documented advantages, a large proportion of students who begin studying a foreign language abandon the effort before reaching a functional level of proficiency. Understanding why so many learners struggle or give up is essential not only for educators and curriculum designers but for anyone who hopes to successfully acquire a new language themselves.", "One of the most frequently cited barriers to language learning is the fear of making mistakes. Unlike subjects such as mathematics or history, where errors can be made silently and corrected privately, language learning requires students to speak aloud, often in front of peers, and to accept that mispronunciation, grammatical errors, and vocabulary gaps are an unavoidable part of the process. For many learners, particularly those from educational cultures that place a high value on accuracy and public performance, the prospect of speaking imperfectly in front of others triggers significant anxiety. This linguistic anxiety — sometimes called \"foreign language anxiety\" in research literature — has been consistently identified as one of the strongest predictors of poor language learning outcomes, capable of suppressing participation, reducing the willingness to practice, and ultimately causing students to disengage entirely from the learning process.", "A second major barrier is the absence of meaningful exposure to the target language outside the classroom. Language acquisition research, drawing on the work of scholars such as Stephen Krashen, has long argued that true fluency develops not primarily through formal instruction but through extensive, comprehensible input — encounters with the language in contexts that are meaningful and slightly above the learner's current level. In a typical school or university setting, formal language instruction may amount to only a few hours per week, a volume of exposure that is simply insufficient to drive the neural changes associated with deep language acquisition. Students who have no access to native speakers, no opportunity to travel to a country where the language is spoken, and no habit of consuming media in the target language are therefore working against a severe structural disadvantage that classroom time alone cannot fully compensate for.", "Motivation is another critical variable, and researchers have drawn an important distinction between two fundamentally different types. Instrumental motivation refers to learning a language for a specific practical purpose — to pass an examination, to meet an academic requirement, or to improve employment prospects. Integrative motivation, by contrast, involves a genuine desire to connect with the culture, people, and literature associated with the target language. Studies have consistently found that learners driven by integrative motivation tend to persist longer, reach higher levels of proficiency, and derive greater satisfaction from the process. Students who are learning a language purely to satisfy an external requirement, with no personal investment in the language itself, frequently find their motivation draining away once the immediate goal — passing a test, for instance — has been achieved.", "The structure and pace of formal language curricula can also create significant barriers, particularly when they are poorly aligned with the actual cognitive demands of language learning. Many traditional curricula prioritize grammatical accuracy and written production at the expense of communicative fluency, producing students who can correctly conjugate a verb in a written exercise but freeze when asked to use the same structure in a spontaneous spoken exchange. The communicative language teaching approach, which emphasizes real-life interaction and meaningful communication over the memorization of rules, has been shown to produce more confident and functional speakers. However, large class sizes, high-stakes testing regimes, and teacher workloads often make it difficult for educators to implement communicative methods consistently in practice.", "Individual differences in aptitude also play a role, though their influence is more nuanced than popular belief often assumes. Certain cognitive abilities — such as phonological sensitivity, the capacity to notice and internalize grammatical patterns, and working memory capacity — do predict language learning success to a meaningful degree, particularly in the early stages of acquisition. However, research also shows that high aptitude without consistent effort and exposure produces far less impressive results than moderate aptitude combined with sustained, motivated practice. In other words, the learner who works methodically and maintains regular contact with the target language will generally outperform a more naturally gifted learner who relies on ability alone.", "Addressing the barriers to foreign language acquisition requires a multi-layered response. At the classroom level, creating a low-anxiety environment in which errors are treated as learning opportunities rather than failures can dramatically increase student participation and willingness to take the communicative risks that language learning demands. At the curriculum level, incorporating authentic materials — films, podcasts, news articles, and social media content in the target language — can begin to bridge the gap between classroom exposure and real-world input. And at the individual level, encouraging students to cultivate a personal relationship with the language — through music, literature, travel, or online communities — may be the single most powerful step toward turning a reluctant learner into a genuinely motivated one."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["A comparison of the most effective language teaching methods used in schools around the world", "The cognitive and career benefits that students gain by achieving fluency in a foreign language", "Research findings that prove motivation is more important than natural aptitude in language learning", "The key obstacles that prevent students from successfully learning a foreign language, and how these might be addressed"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what effect does foreign language anxiety have on students?", options: ["It causes students to focus more on written work and to avoid oral examinations whenever possible.", "It suppresses participation, reduces willingness to practice, and can lead students to disengage from learning entirely.", "It primarily affects students from non-English-speaking backgrounds who are learning English as a foreign language.", "It makes students more cautious and accurate in their written work, even if their spoken performance suffers."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does research drawing on Stephen Krashen's work suggest about how fluency is best developed?", options: ["Students reach fluency faster when they study abroad for at least one full academic year before returning home.", "Formal grammar instruction in a classroom setting is the most reliable path to achieving functional language proficiency.", "Fluency develops most effectively when students memorize large quantities of vocabulary before attempting to speak.", "True fluency develops primarily through extensive exposure to meaningful language input, not through formal instruction alone."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what distinguishes integrative motivation from instrumental motivation in language learning?", options: ["Integrative motivation involves a genuine desire to connect with the culture and people of the target language, while instrumental motivation is driven by practical external goals.", "Integrative motivation is based on passing examinations, while instrumental motivation is linked to cultural interest.", "Integrative motivation is more common among younger learners, while older learners tend to rely on instrumental motivation.", "Instrumental motivation leads to higher proficiency levels, while integrative motivation produces more confident speakers."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"their\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["researchers who study the different types of language learning motivation", "educators who design language curricula around examination outcomes", "students learning a language purely to fulfill an external requirement", "learners driven by integrative motivation who reach higher proficiency levels"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does research suggest about the relationship between natural aptitude and consistent practice in language learning?", options: ["Natural aptitude is the single most important factor in determining how quickly a student learns a new language.", "Aptitude and practice contribute equally to language learning success, and neither is significantly more important than the other.", "A learner with moderate aptitude who practices consistently will generally do better than a highly gifted learner who depends on ability alone.", "Students with low aptitude are unlikely to reach a high level of proficiency regardless of how much effort they invest."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"comprehensible\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["produced by native speakers without any simplification or modification", "written down and available for repeated study at any time", "advanced and challenging enough to stretch the learner beyond their current ability", "able to be understood and processed by the person receiving it"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"internalize\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["to absorb something deeply so that it becomes a natural part of one's own thinking", "to translate something from one language into another automatically", "to record something in writing so that it can be reviewed later", "to reject or filter out information that does not match existing knowledge"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the author's main purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To criticize language teachers for failing to create engaging and communicative lessons in their classrooms", "To argue that formal language education is largely ineffective and should be replaced by immersive travel experiences", "To analyze the reasons why students struggle to learn foreign languages and to suggest ways these difficulties can be overcome", "To persuade students that natural talent is not required to learn a language if they follow the correct study method"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a recommended approach to overcoming barriers to language learning?", options: ["Using authentic materials such as films and podcasts in the target language", "Encouraging students to develop a personal connection to the language through music, literature, or online communities", "Building a classroom culture where mistakes are seen as part of the learning process", "Arranging regular conversation sessions with native speaker exchange partners during class time"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Remarkable Symbiosis of Fiddler Crabs", paragraphs: ["Among the tidal mudflats, salt marshes, and mangrove forests that fringe coastlines across the tropics and subtropics, few creatures are as ecologically consequential as the fiddler crab. Belonging to the genus Uca — a name now largely superseded in recent taxonomic revisions by Gelasimus and related genera — fiddler crabs comprise more than one hundred species distributed across coastal zones from West Africa to the Indo-Pacific and the Americas. Despite their modest size, typically ranging from two to five centimeters in body width, these small crustaceans exert an influence on the ecosystems they inhabit that is profoundly disproportionate to their stature. Their burrowing activity, feeding behavior, and social signaling have each been identified as drivers of ecological processes that extend well beyond the individual crab, shaping the physical, chemical, and biological structure of the habitats in which they reside.", "The most immediately striking feature of the fiddler crab is the extreme asymmetry of the male's claws. In all species, the male possesses one greatly enlarged claw — the major cheliped — that can account for up to half of the animal's total body weight, and one small claw that is used for feeding. The large claw serves no nutritional function whatsoever; it is used exclusively for two purposes: combat with rival males over burrow ownership, and courtship signaling directed at females. The characteristic waving motion of the enlarged claw that gives the group its common name is a species-specific display whose speed, arc, and rhythm are calibrated to the sensory preferences of conspecific females, and which has been shown in laboratory studies to carry information about the male's body condition, burrow quality, and relative dominance within the local population.", "The ecological significance of fiddler crab burrowing is considerable and has been documented across multiple dimensions. Each crab excavates a burrow that may extend to a depth of thirty centimeters or more, and in dense colonies, burrow densities can reach several hundred per square meter of sediment surface. This pervasive bioturbation — the biological disturbance of sediment by living organisms — has measurable effects on sediment chemistry. By introducing oxygen into the otherwise anoxic subsurface layers of mudflat sediment, fiddler crab burrows dramatically alter the redox conditions of the substrate, facilitating the decomposition of organic matter and modifying nutrient cycling processes in ways that benefit the broader marsh and mangrove ecosystem. Studies conducted in salt marshes along the eastern coast of the United States and in mangrove systems in Southeast Asia have found that areas of dense fiddler crab colonization exhibit significantly higher rates of nitrogen cycling and primary productivity compared to equivalent areas from which crabs have been experimentally removed.", "[1] The feeding behavior of fiddler crabs further amplifies their ecological role. Rather than consuming prey, fiddler crabs are deposit feeders — they ingest surface sediment and extract organic matter, bacteria, fungi, and microalgae from it before expelling the processed sediment as small pellets. [2] This selective processing of the sediment surface alters both its organic content and its microbial community structure. Where fiddler crab populations are dense, the microbial assemblage of the surface sediment has been shown to differ substantially from that of adjacent, crab-free areas, with implications for decomposition rates, nutrient availability, and the palatability of the habitat for other deposit-feeding invertebrates. [3] The crabs thus function not merely as consumers within the food web but as engineers of the microbial landscape across which other organisms must navigate. [4]", "The reproductive ecology of fiddler crabs has yielded some of the most intriguing findings in behavioral biology. Female fiddler crabs are highly selective in their choice of mating partner, and the criteria they apply are more nuanced than simple preference for the largest claw. Research conducted on species including Uca mjoebergi in Australia has demonstrated that females will sometimes preferentially approach burrows previously vacated by a neighboring female — a behavior interpreted as indirect mate copying, in which the choice of another individual serves as social information about burrow or claw quality. Males, for their part, will occasionally construct elaborate sand structures adjacent to their burrow entrances — known as hoods or pillars — that appear to function as acoustic and visual signals amplifying the attractiveness of the burrow to passing females. The sophistication of this signaling system has led some researchers to describe fiddler crab courtship as one of the most complex visual communication systems documented in any crustacean.", "Conservation concern for fiddler crab populations has grown in parallel with the recognition of their ecological importance. The coastal habitats on which these crabs depend — mangrove forests, salt marshes, and tidal mudflats — are among the most threatened ecosystems on the planet, facing pressure from land reclamation, aquaculture expansion, coastal urbanization, and the sea level rise associated with climate change. Because fiddler crabs serve as both ecosystem engineers and prey items for a wide range of shorebirds and larger crustaceans, their decline in any given coastal system can trigger cascading effects that diminish the overall resilience and productivity of that system. Researchers working in degraded mangrove systems in Malaysia and the Philippines have documented measurable declines in sediment aeration and nutrient cycling following substantial reductions in fiddler crab density, findings that underscore the difficulty of restoring full ecosystem function through replanting programs alone.", "The study of fiddler crabs thus offers a compelling illustration of a principle that ecologists have long sought to communicate to policymakers and the wider public: that the ecological importance of a species is not correlated with its size, its charisma, or its visibility to the casual observer. The conservation of coastal ecosystems depends as much on the preservation of their small, inconspicuous engineers as on the protection of the large, emblematic species that typically attract public and political attention. In this sense, the fiddler crab serves as both a subject of scientific fascination and a symbol of the often-overlooked complexity upon which coastal ecosystem health ultimately rests."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Despite their small size, fiddler crabs play critical ecological roles in coastal ecosystems and their conservation is essential to maintaining those ecosystems.", "Fiddler crabs are primarily studied for their unusual physical appearance and complex courtship displays.", "Fiddler crabs are the most studied crustacean species in the world because of their unusual claw asymmetry.", "The primary threat to fiddler crab populations is overharvesting by local fishing communities in tropical coastal regions."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, for what two purposes does the male fiddler crab use its enlarged claw?", options: ["Fighting rival males for burrow ownership and signaling to attract females.", "Regulating its body temperature and communicating danger to other crabs.", "Gathering food and defending its territory from predators.", "Excavating burrows and transporting sediment away from the burrow entrance."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does fiddler crab burrowing benefit the chemistry of mudflat sediment?", options: ["By channeling oxygen into otherwise oxygen-poor subsurface layers, which alters redox conditions and supports nutrient cycling.", "By filtering pollutants from tidal water through a network of interconnected burrow passages.", "By depositing nutrient-rich organic material collected from the water surface into the subsurface sediment layers.", "By compacting the sediment surface to prevent erosion during tidal fluctuations."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"calibrated\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["randomly varied to avoid predictable patterns", "gradually learned through repeated social exposure", "performed instinctively without prior learning", "finely adjusted to match specific requirements"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the behavior known as \"indirect mate copying\" involve in female fiddler crabs?", options: ["Females mimicking the waving display of males to signal their readiness to mate.", "Females choosing a male based on the size of his enlarged claw relative to his body weight.", "Females constructing sand structures near their own burrows to attract the attention of passing males.", "Females approaching burrows that were recently vacated by another female, using that prior choice as social information."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following threats to fiddler crab habitats is NOT mentioned?", options: ["Land reclamation for urban and industrial development.", "Sea level rise linked to climate change.", "Expansion of aquaculture operations in coastal zones.", "Increased salinity levels caused by reduced freshwater inflow from rivers."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"pervasive\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["occurring in isolated patches across the sediment surface", "spreading widely and present throughout an area", "detectable only through specialized laboratory analysis", "causing irreversible damage to the substrate"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 1 refers to _______.", options: ["the ecological processes driven by fiddler crab activity.", "the coastlines across the tropics and subtropics where fiddler crabs are found.", "the tidal mudflats, salt marshes, and mangrove forests described at the opening of the paragraph.", "fiddler crabs."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 3 and 6, what can be inferred about the difficulty of restoring degraded mangrove ecosystems?", options: ["Mangrove restoration is straightforward once fiddler crab populations are reintroduced, as the crabs rapidly rebuild sediment chemistry.", "Restoration efforts have been largely successful in Malaysia and the Philippines because of strong government investment.", "Degraded mangrove systems recover naturally within a decade once threats such as aquaculture expansion are removed.", "The loss of fiddler crabs leads to declines in sediment aeration and nutrient cycling that replanting programs alone cannot fully restore."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added to paragraph 4? \"In this way, each feeding bout simultaneously functions as an act of habitat modification.\"", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Volcanoes: Architects of the Earth's Surface", paragraphs: ["The popular imagination tends to frame volcanic eruptions primarily as instruments of destruction — catastrophic events that bury landscapes under lava and ash, displace communities, and periodically alter the global climate. This framing, while not inaccurate, captures only one dimension of a far more complex geological reality. Volcanoes are not merely agents of devastation; they are among the most consequential creative forces in Earth's geological and biological history. The land masses on which human civilizations have developed, the soils that support the majority of the world's agricultural production, the mineral deposits that underpin modern industrial economies, and even the gases that first constituted the primordial atmosphere are all, in significant part, volcanic in origin. [1] Understanding volcanism in its full scope therefore demands a conceptual shift — from a hazard to be mitigated to an Earth-shaping process to be comprehended. [2]", "At the most fundamental level, volcanic activity is the surface expression of the planet's internal heat engine. The Earth's interior retains enormous quantities of heat from its formation approximately 4.5 billion years ago, supplemented by the ongoing decay of radioactive isotopes within the mantle and crust. This heat drives convection currents in the semi-molten mantle, which in turn propel the movement of tectonic plates at the surface. Where plates diverge — as occurs along the mid-ocean ridge system that extends more than 65,000 kilometers across the ocean floor — magma wells up to fill the gap, continuously generating new oceanic crust. Where one plate subducts beneath another, the descending slab releases water and other volatile compounds into the overlying mantle wedge, lowering its melting point and generating the magma that feeds the explosive stratovolcanoes characteristically found along subduction zone arcs, including the Pacific Ring of Fire.", "The constructive role of volcanic activity in building Earth's landmasses is most dramatically illustrated by the formation of oceanic island chains. The Hawaiian Islands represent perhaps the most extensively studied example of this process: a sequence of volcanic islands and seamounts extending for more than 2,500 kilometers across the central Pacific, each formed by the passage of the Pacific Plate over a stationary mantle plume — a column of anomalously hot material rising from deep within the mantle. The oldest islands in the chain, situated to the northwest, have been sufficiently eroded and submerged to form low atolls and seamounts, while the youngest island, Hawai'i itself, remains volcanically active and continues to grow through ongoing lava effusion. The progression of ages along the chain serves as a precise record of plate motion, allowing geologists to calculate the velocity of the Pacific Plate over geological time with considerable accuracy.", "Volcanic soils — derived from the chemical weathering of lava flows and the deposition of volcanic ash — are among the most fertile on Earth. The rapid breakdown of basaltic and andesitic rock releases high concentrations of phosphorus, potassium, calcium, and trace elements that are essential to plant growth. The volcanic soils of the island of Java in Indonesia, the highlands of Ethiopia, and the slopes of Mount Etna in Sicily have supported dense human populations for millennia precisely because of their exceptional agricultural productivity. This agricultural abundance has historically induced settlement and population growth in the immediate vicinity of active volcanoes, despite the hazards this proximity entails — a pattern that demographers and disaster risk researchers describe as the \"volcano magnet\" effect. By some estimates, nearly ten percent of the world's population currently lives within one hundred kilometers of a potentially active volcano, with a disproportionate concentration in agriculturally dependent societies in the developing world.", "The atmospheric and climatic effects of large-magnitude volcanic eruptions have been extensively documented and in some cases appear to have influenced the trajectory of human history. The 1815 eruption of Mount Tambora in present-day Indonesia — the most energetic volcanic event in recorded history — injected an estimated 150 to 200 cubic kilometers of material into the stratosphere, including enormous quantities of sulfur dioxide that converted to sulfate aerosols reflecting incoming solar radiation back into space. The result was a measurable reduction in global mean surface temperature of approximately 0.5 degrees Celsius that persisted for two to three years and produced widespread crop failures in Europe and North America during the so-called \"Year Without a Summer\" of 1816. Historical research has linked these agricultural disruptions to famine, civil unrest, and population displacement across multiple continents, illustrating how a single geological event can generate cascading societal consequences at a global scale.", "The deep-time perspective on volcanism reveals an even more fundamental contribution: the role of volcanic outgassing in generating and maintaining the Earth's atmosphere and oceans. In the planet's early history, volcanic eruptions continuously released water vapor, carbon dioxide, nitrogen, and sulfur dioxide from the interior. The condensation of outgassed water vapor is believed to have been the primary source of Earth's early oceans, and the carbon dioxide emitted by volcanic activity has played a central role in regulating the long-term carbon cycle over geological timescales through its interaction with the silicate weathering feedback mechanism. Without the sustained outgassing maintained by ongoing volcanism throughout Earth's history, the planet's surface conditions would be radically different — and the emergence of complex life, which depends on liquid water and atmospheric stability, would arguably not have been possible in the form it has taken.", "The reconceptualization of volcanoes as architects of the Earth's surface rather than solely as hazards thus rests on a body of evidence drawn from petrology, geochemistry, palaeoclimatology, and historical demography. This reframing does not diminish the genuine risks that volcanic activity poses to human populations living in proximity to active systems; it does, however, insist on the necessity of understanding those risks within a broader geological and evolutionary context. The same processes that occasionally devastate communities have, over billions of years, created the conditions that make those communities possible in the first place — a paradox that lies at the heart of the Earth sciences and invites a more sophisticated relationship between human societies and the dynamic planet they inhabit."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best states the central argument of the passage?", options: ["Volcanic eruptions are primarily destructive events whose benefits to human civilization are marginal and historically overstated.", "Volcanoes are not only agents of destruction but fundamental constructive forces that have shaped the Earth's landmasses, soils, atmosphere, and conditions for life over geological time.", "The risks posed by volcanic activity are now fully understood, and modern societies have sufficient tools to mitigate them effectively.", "The geological study of volcanoes is important mainly because it allows scientists to predict eruptions and protect nearby populations."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what process generates the magma that feeds explosive stratovolcanoes along subduction zones?", options: ["The fracturing of continental crust by tectonic pressure, which allows pre-existing magma to rise toward the surface.", "The direct melting of oceanic crust as it descends into the hot lower mantle.", "The accumulation of heat from radioactive decay specifically within the subducting plate itself.", "The release of water and volatile compounds from the subducting slab into the overlying mantle wedge, which lowers the mantle's melting point."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the sequence of ages along the Hawaiian island chain allow geologists to determine?", options: ["The velocity at which the Pacific Plate has moved over geological time.", "The depth and temperature of the magma chamber currently feeding the island of Hawai'i.", "The rate at which volcanic islands erode once they have moved away from the hotspot.", "The chemical composition of the mantle plume beneath the central Pacific Ocean."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"progression\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a random distribution of geological features across a wide area", "an ordered sequence in which each element relates to those adjacent to it", "a gradual increase in the size of volcanic structures over time", "a sudden shift in direction caused by changing tectonic forces"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was the primary mechanism by which the 1815 Tambora eruption caused global cooling?", options: ["The eruption released vast quantities of carbon dioxide that trapped heat in the lower atmosphere, disrupting normal wind patterns.", "Lava flows from the eruption covered large areas of reflective ocean surface, reducing the amount of solar energy absorbed by the Earth.", "The eruption produced a dense cloud of volcanic ash that blocked sunlight over the Indonesian archipelago for several years.", "Sulfur dioxide injected into the stratosphere converted to sulfate aerosols that reflected incoming solar radiation back into space."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a constructive contribution of volcanic activity to the Earth?", options: ["The generation of fertile agricultural soils through the weathering of volcanic rock.", "The creation of deep-sea trenches along the boundaries of diverging tectonic plates.", "The provision of gases that contributed to the formation of the Earth's early atmosphere.", "The formation of oceanic island chains through lava effusion over mantle plumes."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"induced\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["gradually discouraged through repeated exposure to risk", "statistically predicted based on historical population data", "formally required by government land use policy", "brought about or encouraged as a result of a specific condition"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The phrase \"its formation\" in paragraph 2 refers to the formation of _______.", options: ["the Earth itself.", "the semi-molten mantle layer beneath the tectonic plates.", "the mid-ocean ridge system extending across the ocean floor.", "the system of radioactive isotopes within the planet's crust."], answer: 0 }, { q: "Based on paragraph 6, what does the author imply about the relationship between volcanic outgassing and the possibility of complex life on Earth?", options: ["Volcanic outgassing had positive effects on atmospheric formation but ultimately destabilized the carbon cycle in ways that nearly prevented complex life from emerging.", "Volcanic outgassing was a minor factor in Earth's early development compared to the role of meteor impacts in delivering water to the planet's surface.", "The contribution of volcanic outgassing to atmospheric development ended once plate tectonics stabilized approximately two billion years ago.", "The outgassing of water vapor and carbon dioxide by early volcanoes was likely essential to creating the atmospheric and oceanic conditions that made complex life possible."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added to paragraph 1? \"The sheer scale of this constructive output challenges any account of volcanism that reduces it to a story of destruction alone.\"", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
  ] },
];
